begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-002','[["Methodology as a structured delivery approach","A team has templates and software but no agreed sequence of processes, roles, decision points or rules for managing the project.","Adopt and tailor a coherent project management methodology that integrates processes, roles, governance and supporting tools.","Treat the available software as the methodology because tools define how projects are governed.","Allow every team member to follow a different personal process.","Postpone methodology decisions until the project is ready to close.","A methodology is an organised approach that guides how work is initiated, planned, delivered, controlled and closed; tools support but do not replace it."],["Tailoring methodology to context","Management insists that a small, low-risk internal improvement must use the same documentation and approval structure as a multibillion-naira infrastructure programme.","Tailor the selected methodology to the project''s scale, complexity, risk, regulatory environment and stakeholder needs.","Apply every control unchanged because tailoring automatically weakens governance.","Remove all governance because the project is internal.","Choose the methodology solely according to the project manager''s certification.","Methodology tailoring preserves necessary control while avoiding unnecessary burden and should reflect project context."],["Waterfall suitability","The scope is stable, regulatory approvals require complete design documentation, and construction must follow a largely sequential order.","Use a predictive or Waterfall approach with controlled phase progression and formal change management.","Use an unplanned continuous-flow approach with no approved baseline.","Start construction before requirements and design are substantially defined.","Replace all documentation with short daily conversations.","Predictive approaches suit work with stable requirements, sequential dependencies and strong documentation or regulatory needs."],["Waterfall limitations","A digital product has uncertain user needs and stakeholders expect frequent demonstrations and changes as they learn.","Avoid relying on a rigid Waterfall-only approach and use an iterative or Agile method that enables feedback and adaptation.","Freeze all requirements before users see any working product.","Delay stakeholder feedback until final deployment.","Treat every new insight as noncompliance rather than learning.","Rigid predictive delivery is less suitable when requirements are uncertain and value depends on frequent feedback."],["Critical Path Method application","A schedule contains well-defined activities and dependencies, and management needs to know which delays will directly affect the completion date.","Apply the Critical Path Method to calculate activity timing, float and the path controlling project duration.","Use a stakeholder power-interest grid to calculate completion time.","Rank activities only by cost and assume the most expensive is critical.","Use a Kanban board without estimating dependencies or durations.","CPM analyses network logic and durations to identify the critical path and schedule flexibility."],["Agile mindset and iterative delivery","The team can deliver usable increments every few weeks and the customer can provide frequent feedback on evolving requirements.","Use an Agile approach that delivers incrementally, welcomes learning and prioritises customer value.","Prevent all requirement changes after project initiation.","Wait until all features are complete before showing the customer any output.","Measure progress mainly by the volume of documents produced.","Agile is appropriate where iterative delivery, feedback, collaboration and responsiveness to change improve value."],["Scrum framework","A cross-functional product team needs a timeboxed cadence, clear accountabilities, a prioritised backlog and regular inspection and adaptation.","Use Scrum with a Product Owner, Scrum Master, Developers, Sprints, events and defined artefacts.","Use Waterfall stages but rename each stage a Sprint.","Appoint the sponsor as the only person allowed to perform team work.","Use an unlimited work queue with no timebox, review or retrospective.","Scrum provides defined accountabilities, events and artefacts for iterative product delivery in Sprints."],["Kanban flow management","A support and maintenance team receives unpredictable work continuously and wants to reduce bottlenecks and improve flow without fixed Sprints.","Visualise the workflow, limit work in progress and manage flow using Kanban.","Start every request immediately so no item waits in the queue.","Create one annual batch and deliver all requests together.","Measure success only by how many tasks are assigned, not completed.","Kanban supports continuous flow through visualisation, work-in-progress limits and active bottleneck management."],["Lean methodology","A process contains repeated handoffs, waiting, rework and features that customers do not value.","Apply Lean principles to identify value, remove waste, improve flow and pursue continuous improvement.","Add more approvals to every step regardless of risk.","Increase work in progress so each person appears busy.","Retain non-value-adding activities because they have always existed.","Lean focuses on customer value, elimination of waste, flow and continuous improvement."],["Hybrid methodology","A project has fixed regulatory and construction milestones, but its digital monitoring component requires iterative user testing and adaptation.","Design a hybrid approach that governs stable components predictively and manages uncertain components iteratively.","Force every component into one method even when its uncertainty and constraints differ.","Run two unrelated projects with no integrated governance or dependencies.","Use Agile terminology while keeping all work sequential and feedback-free.","Hybrid delivery deliberately combines methods so each component is managed according to its characteristics while remaining integrated."],["DUCAP contextual methodology","A complex Nigerian project needs controlled governance, adaptive delivery, cultural sensitivity, sustainability and strong stakeholder integration.","Consider DUCAP and tailor its controlled, agile and context-sensitive elements to the project.","Use an imported framework unchanged and ignore local political, cultural and community realities.","Remove governance controls so the team can respond quickly.","Focus only on initial delivery and exclude post-delivery sustainability.","DUCAP integrates controlled governance with agility and explicit attention to context, stakeholders, sustainability and value."],["PRINCE2 principles and continued business justification","A project continues consuming funds although the expected benefits have disappeared and no one has reviewed whether the business case remains valid.","Apply continued business justification and escalate a decision to stop, redirect or rejustify the project.","Continue because an approved project must always reach its original end.","Replace the business case with the latest schedule report.","Judge viability only by whether the team is busy.","PRINCE2 requires continued business justification throughout the project, not only at approval."],["PRINCE2 management by stages and exception","Senior management wants control of a high-risk project without approving every routine team decision.","Divide the project into management stages, set tolerances and escalate only forecast exceptions beyond delegated limits.","Require the project board to approve every daily task.","Remove tolerances so no one knows when escalation is required.","Allow unlimited deviation until the final stage.","Management by stages and by exception provides proportionate control through delegated tolerances and defined escalation."],["PMBOK as a body of knowledge","A manager treats PMBOK as a mandatory step-by-step method that must be followed identically on every project.","Use PMBOK as a body of principles, domains, processes, tools and good practices to be selected and tailored for the context.","Use PMBOK only as a software package for schedule calculation.","Ignore organisational methodology because PMBOK automatically replaces governance.","Apply every possible process and document regardless of value.","PMBOK is a body of knowledge and guidance, not a single prescriptive methodology; practices must be tailored."],["Methodology selection criteria","A sponsor asks the team to choose a methodology before analysing requirement stability, risk, compliance, team capability, stakeholder access or delivery urgency.","Assess the project''s characteristics and constraints, compare suitable approaches and document the selection and tailoring rationale.","Choose the newest methodology because popularity proves suitability.","Select the approach used on the last project without comparison.","Let the vendor choose secretly after contract award.","Methodology selection should be evidence-based and consider uncertainty, complexity, regulation, stakeholder engagement, capability and value-delivery needs."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
